('7a1b5eff-3026-4988-b5fd-5e42c8489b1c','image','3,625m of climbing in 140.7km. That is the elite men''s course at the UCI Gravel World Championships in Nannup, Western Australia, on Sunday 11 October. The women race 123.1km with 3,100m of climbing on Saturday 10 October. All races start at 10:00 local time, which is 12:30 in Adelaide.

What the UCI says about the course: at least 80% gravel, a 9km flat paved start, climbs that are never long but follow each other quickly with little recovery, and a final 27km loop with two climbs.

What that means for the bike (our read, using BikeFinder''s Gravel Finder data):
Gearing: roughly 26m of climbing per kilometre on the men''s course. The median lowest gear across the 31 gravel bikes with gearing data is 0.88. The Specialized Diverge 4 Comp Alloy ($3,500) runs 0.80, and the Cube Nuroad Pro ($1,999) matches it.
Tyres: loose, steep climbs reward volume, the paved start rewards speed. A bike with room to move, like the Liv Devote Advanced 2 at 53mm ($3,699), keeps the choice open.
Weight: repeated steep pitches favour a light bike. The cheapest 9kg bike we track is the Cube Nuroad C:62 Pro at $4,999.

Specs and prices from bikefinder.au/gravel, checked 4 October 2026. Researched from manufacturer and retailer sources, not physical tests.

