('7a1b5eff-3026-4988-b5fd-5e42c8489b1c','image','1x gives up the top end, not the climbs. We compared gearing across the gravel bikes in BikeFinder''s Gravel Finder that list their ratios: 13 one-by bikes and 14 two-by bikes.

Lowest gear (lower is easier uphill): 1x averages 0.86, 2x averages 0.89. A tie in practice.
Top gear: 1x averages 3.92, 2x averages 4.26, about 8% less.
Our maths: on 45mm tyres at 90rpm, that is roughly 46km/h of top speed for one-by versus 50km/h for two-by.

So the common belief that two-by climbs easier is not what the data says. The real trade is at the top end. For loose, steep gravel racing like this weekend''s UCI Gravel World Championships in Nannup, one-by gives up very little. If your rides include long fast road sections, two-by keeps a bit more speed in reserve.

Ratios come from bikefinder.au/gravel, derived from the published chainring and cassette on each bike, checked 4 October 2026. Researched from manufacturer and retailer sources, not physical tests, and can vary by model year.

One-by or two-by on your gravel bike? #gravelbike #bikefinder',array['https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media@6d84380/posts/2026-10-05/bikefinder/bf-06-1x-vs-2x.jpg'],'2026-10-07T07:30:00+00','queued','w1005-bikefinder-2026-10-07-pm-fb','roxy',null),
('32a573bb-697e-4c12-b64a-290a5991bbfc','image','1x gives up the top end, not the climbs. We compared the gearing on every gravel bike in our Gravel Finder that lists its ratios: 13 one-by bikes and 14 two-by bikes.

Lowest gear: 0.86 on average for 1x, 0.89 for 2x. Basically a tie, and 1x is the lower of the two on average.
Top gear: 3.92 for 1x, 4.26 for 2x. That is about 8% less.
In speed terms (our maths, 45mm tyres, 90rpm): you spin out at roughly 46km/h on 1x and 50km/h on 2x.

Spec trap: do not pick a drivetrain because you think one-by is easier for climbing. It is a top end choice. For loose, steep Nannup style racing, one-by gives up almost nothing. For fast road legs it gives up a bit.

Method: BikeFinder Gravel Finder, ratios derived from chainring and cassette, 27 bikes, checked 4 Oct 2026. Researched specs, not ride tests.

