('7a1b5eff-3026-4988-b5fd-5e42c8489b1c','image','Same rider. Three geometries. We took the median numbers across 146 road, gravel and mountain bikes in BikeFinder''s Geometry Lab to see what changes as the tyres get fatter.

Road (48 bikes): head angle 72.8 degrees, chainstay 410mm, wheelbase 992mm, tyre clearance 32mm.
Gravel (33 bikes): 71.0 degrees, 430mm, 1,046mm, 45mm.
Mountain (65 bikes): 67.0 degrees, 435mm, 1,163mm, 61mm.
Median listed weight: road 8.6kg (25 bikes), gravel 10.5kg (15 bikes), mountain 14.8kg (24 bikes).

The takeaway: gravel sits much closer to road on head angle and wheelbase than to mountain bikes, but it is already 20mm longer in the chainstay and 13mm wider in tyre room. The biggest jump from gravel to mountain is the head angle (4 degrees slacker) and the wheelbase (117mm longer).

Method: bikefinder.au/geometry. One published size per bike (mostly M or 54 to 56) so wheelbase is indicative, and the mountain category mixes cross country and trail bikes. Specs researched from manufacturer and retailer sources, checked 4 October 2026, not ride tests.

Which of the three do you ride most? #geometry #bikefinder',array['https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media@6d84380/posts/2026-10-05/bikefinder/bf-07-geometry-1.jpg','https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media@6d84380/posts/2026-10-05/bikefinder/bf-07-geometry-2.jpg','https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media@6d84380/posts/2026-10-05/bikefinder/bf-07-geometry-3.jpg','https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media@6d84380/posts/2026-10-05/bikefinder/bf-07-geometry-4.jpg','https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media@6d84380/posts/2026-10-05/bikefinder/bf-07-geometry-5.jpg'],'2026-10-07T22:00:00+00','queued','w1005-bikefinder-2026-10-08-am-fb','roxy',null),
('32a573bb-697e-4c12-b64a-290a5991bbfc','image','Same rider. Three geometries. We took the median numbers across 146 road, gravel and mountain bikes in our Geometry Lab to see what actually changes as the tyres get fatter.

Road (48 bikes): 72.8 degree head angle, 410mm chainstay, 992mm wheelbase, 32mm tyre clearance.
Gravel (33 bikes): 71.0 degrees, 430mm, 1,046mm, 45mm.
Mountain (65 bikes): 67.0 degrees, 435mm, 1,163mm, 61mm.
Weight (median listed): road 8.6kg, gravel 10.5kg, mountain 14.8kg.

Gravel sits between the two, much nearer road on head angle and wheelbase. The biggest jumps come at the mountain bike end: 4 degrees slacker and 117mm longer in the wheelbase. The UCI Gravel World Championships in Nannup this weekend are raced on bikes from that middle ground.

Method: BikeFinder Geometry Lab, 167 bikes with a full geometry profile, 146 in these three categories, one published size per bike (mostly size M or 54 to 56), median of each field, checked 4 Oct 2026. Researched specs, not ride tests.

