('dfe6d125-63b5-46f6-abf4-9b69d29375ad','image','700 km home. Petrol or plug? Heading back from the long weekend, a petrol car using 7.0 L/100km burns 49 litres. At the 237.1c/L five capital city average the ACCC reported for 23 September, that is $116. An EV using 16 kWh/100km needs 112 kWh. Charged at home on the Ausgrid flat rate default market offer (33.1c/kWh), that is $37. About $79 back on one trip. Your car and your tariff will move those numbers, so what do you pay per kWh at home? #evibe #evcharging',array['https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media@6d84380/posts/2026-10-05/evibe/p01-mon-am.jpg'],'2026-10-04T22:00:00+00','queued','w1005-evibe-2026-10-05-am-fb','roxy',null),
('dfe6d125-63b5-46f6-abf4-9b69d29375ad','image','The EV tax break is changing. On 5 May 2026 the Treasurer and Minister Bowen announced three phases for the FBT exemption on electric cars. Until 31 March 2027 nothing changes: full exemption for EVs under the $91,661 luxury car tax limit. From 1 April 2027 to 1 April 2029 the full break applies only to EVs costing $75,000 or less, and EVs above that (up to the limit) get a 25% discount on their payable FBT. From 1 April 2029 every EV under the limit gets the 25% discount. Existing leases are not affected. If you are weighing a novated lease, ask your provider which phase your car lands in. This is general information, not tax advice. Does the $75,000 line change what you would buy? #evibe #novatedlease',array['https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media@6d84380/posts/2026-10-05/evibe/p02-mon-pm-s1.jpg','https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media@6d84380/posts/2026-10-05/evibe/p02-mon-pm-s2.jpg','https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media@6d84380/posts/2026-10-05/evibe/p02-mon-pm-s3.jpg'],'2026-10-05T07:30:00+00','queued','w1005-evibe-2026-10-05-pm-fb','roxy',null),
('dfe6d125-63b5-46f6-abf4-9b69d29375ad','reel','Same power. Two prices. On the 2026/27 default market offer time of use tariff in the Energex region (south east Queensland), electricity costs 6.98c per kWh from 11am to 4pm and 47.79c per kWh from 4pm to 9pm, GST included. Charging an EV that uses 16 kWh per 100 km, 100 km costs $1.12 at midday and $7.65 in the evening peak. That is $6.53 more for the same 100 km. Your retailer''s plan will differ, so check which hours are cheap on yours. Do you charge at midday or overnight? #evibe #evcharging',array['https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media@6d84380/posts/2026-10-05/evibe/p03-tue-am-reel.mp4'],'2026-10-05T22:00:00+00','queued','w1005-evibe-2026-10-06-am-fb','roxy',null),
('dfe6d125-63b5-46f6-abf4-9b69d29375ad','image','"EV batteries die after 8 years." Verdict: false. Geotab''s 2025 analysis of more than 22,700 EVs across 21 models found an average loss of 2.3% of capacity a year, which projects to 81.6% of the original capacity after eight years. Wear depends on how you charge: Geotab measured 1.5% a year for the lowest power charging group and up to 3.0% a year for vehicles using 100 kW plus DC fast charging a lot. Hot climates add about 0.4% a year. Do you charge at home or at public fast chargers most of the time? #evibe #evbattery',array['https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media@6d84380/posts/2026-10-05/evibe/p04-tue-pm.jpg'],'2026-10-06T07:30:00+00','queued','w1005-evibe-2026-10-06-pm-fb','roxy',null),
('dfe6d125-63b5-46f6-abf4-9b69d29375ad','image','6 kW of solar. What does it cost? The Solar Choice Price Index for 1 October 2026 puts the national average for a fully installed 6 kW system at $6,330, after the STC discount and including GST. Perth is cheapest at $5,030, then Adelaide at $5,320, Canberra $5,700, Sydney $5,750, Melbourne $5,810, Brisbane $6,210, Hobart $6,950 and Darwin $9,890. Prices vary with panel and inverter brand, so get three quotes and ask what is included. What was your quote? #evibe #solarpower',array['https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media@6d84380/posts/2026-10-05/evibe/p05-wed-am-s1.jpg','https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media@6d84380/posts/2026-10-05/evibe/p05-wed-am-s2.jpg','https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media@6d84380/posts/2026-10-05/evibe/p05-wed-am-s3.jpg','https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media@6d84380/posts/2026-10-05/evibe/p05-wed-am-s4.jpg'],'2026-10-06T22:00:00+00','queued','w1005-evibe-2026-10-07-am-fb','roxy',null),
('dfe6d125-63b5-46f6-abf4-9b69d29375ad','image','Charge outside the peak. Keep $138. A Sydney household on Ausgrid driving 12,000 km a year in an EV that uses 16 kWh per 100 km needs about 1,920 kWh. On the 2026/27 flat rate default market offer (33.14c per kWh) that is $636. On the time of use default market offer the off-peak rate is 24.04c per kWh, so the same charging costs $462, $175 less. Time of use has a daily supply charge 10.2c higher, which is about $37 a year, so the net saving is around $138. The peak rate is 60.20c per kWh from 3pm to 9pm, though Ausgrid has no peak period in April, May, September or October. Which tariff are you on? #evibe #evcharging',array['https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media@6d84380/posts/2026-10-05/evibe/p06-wed-pm.jpg'],'2026-10-07T07:30:00+00','queued','w1005-evibe-2026-10-07-pm-fb','roxy',null),
('dfe6d125-63b5-46f6-abf4-9b69d29375ad','image','Battery rebate drops on 1 January. The Cheaper Home Batteries discount is set by the STC factor on your install date. It is 6.8 for May to December 2026 and falls to 5.7 for January to June 2027, then 5.2 from July 2027. For a 10 kWh battery that is 68 certificates now and 57 from 1 January. At the fixed $40 (ex GST) STC clearing house price, that is $2,720 versus $2,280, so $440 less discount for the same battery. The government says the changes are meant to keep the discount at around 30% as battery prices fall, so check what your quote shows and ask your installer for the install date they can commit to. Thinking of a battery? #evibe #homebattery',array['https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media@6d84380/posts/2026-10-05/evibe/p07-thu-am.jpg'],'2026-10-07T22:00:00+00','queued','w1005-evibe-2026-10-08-am-fb','roxy',null),
('dfe6d125-63b5-46f6-abf4-9b69d29375ad','reel','Your bill, sliced. On a $2,000 power bill, the poles and wires take roughly $780 to $1,080 (39 to 54% of the default market offer price, depending on region), wholesale energy $600 to $820 (30 to 41%) and retail costs $140 to $320 (7 to 16%), with a 6% retail margin and 2 to 3% for green schemes on top. Shares are from the AER''s final 2026/27 Default Market Offer determination and vary by region. Which slice surprised you? #evibe #energybills',array['https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media@6d84380/posts/2026-10-05/evibe/p09-fri-am-reel.mp4'],'2026-10-08T22:00:00+00','queued','w1005-evibe-2026-10-09-am-fb','roxy',null),
('dfe6d125-63b5-46f6-abf4-9b69d29375ad','image','Myth tested: "Solar switches off in winter." Verdict: false. Utility scale solar hit a record share of generation in every NEM state except South Australia during July 2026, and curtailed (wasted) solar fell to 80 GWh from 114 GWh in July 2025, according to Rystad Energy''s monthly update. This is farm output, not rooftop, but the same sun is on your roof. Have you noticed your own winter output? #evibe #solarpower',array['https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media@6d84380/posts/2026-10-05/evibe/p10-fri-pm.jpg'],'2026-10-09T07:30:00+00','queued','w1005-evibe-2026-10-09-pm-fb','roxy',null),
('dfe6d125-63b5-46f6-abf4-9b69d29375ad','image','Driving to the mountain? Sydney to Bathurst and back is about 400 km. In a petrol car using 7.0 L/100 km at 237.1c a litre (ACCC five city average, 23 Sep 2026), that is 28 litres and $66.39. In an EV using 16 kWh/100 km charged at home on the Ausgrid flat rate default offer (33.14c a kWh), it is 64 kWh and $21.21. That is $45.18 less for the same trip. The Bathurst 1000 runs 8 to 11 October with race day on Sunday 11 October. Public charging prices differ, so this is home charging only. Are you making the trip? #evibe #bathurst1000',array['https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media@6d84380/posts/2026-10-05/evibe/p11-sat-am-s1.jpg','https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media@6d84380/posts/2026-10-05/evibe/p11-sat-am-s2.jpg','https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media@6d84380/posts/2026-10-05/evibe/p11-sat-am-s3.jpg','https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media@6d84380/posts/2026-10-05/evibe/p11-sat-am-s4.jpg'],'2026-10-09T22:00:00+00','queued','w1005-evibe-2026-10-10-am-fb','roxy',null),
('dfe6d125-63b5-46f6-abf4-9b69d29375ad','image','How long could your EV run a fridge? On paper, 63 days. Take the Energy Rating example of a 3 star fridge using 349 kWh a year (0.96 kWh a day) and a 60 kWh battery: 60 / 0.96 is about 63 days. A 6 star fridge at 187 kWh a year (0.51 kWh a day) stretches to about 117 days. Real life is shorter: conversion losses, a reserve you need to drive on, and your car must support V2L (power out) with a safe connection. The 60 kWh battery is an example, not your car. What would you keep running in a blackout? #evibe #v2l',array['https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media@6d84380/posts/2026-10-05/evibe/p12-sat-pm.jpg'],'2026-10-10T07:30:00+00','queued','w1005-evibe-2026-10-10-pm-fb','roxy',null),
('dfe6d125-63b5-46f6-abf4-9b69d29375ad','image','Is your plan beating the default? The Default Market Offer (DMO) is the AER''s price cap on default electricity plans in NSW, South East Queensland and South Australia, and it is also the comparison price for market offers. For 2026/27 the residential flat rate DMO is $1,899 a year on Ausgrid (3,900 kWh), $2,328 on Endeavour Energy (4,900 kWh), $2,604 on Essential Energy (4,600 kWh), $1,988 on Energex (4,600 kWh) and $2,334 on SA Power Networks (4,000 kWh), all including GST. Your usage and tariff will differ, so check your own bill against your region. What does your plan cost for the same usage? #evibe #energybills',array['https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/tbhglobal/cartell-social-media@6d84380/posts/2026-10-05/evibe/p14-sun-pm.jpg'],'2026-10-11T07:30:00+00','queued','w1005-evibe-2026-10-11-pm-fb','roxy',null),
('87db4aca-36a7-4fb1-a590-9e2a143fb42a','image','Heading home from the long weekend? Pull over early.

A few kilometres after you set off, stop somewhere safe and check the coupling, safety chains, brake and light plug connections and tyres. Then repeat the check every 2 to 3 hours on the way. That is straight from the Queensland Government''s Safe Towing guide.

Been caught out by a loose connection before? Tell us below. Send this to whoever is towing home today.

